  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2033}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
